# Files Git must hand over byte for byte.
#
# Windows checkouts default to core.autocrlf=true, and Git guesses whether a file
# is text. It guessed wrong on the sample PDFs -- they are mostly ASCII with no
# early NUL byte -- and rewrote every LF inside them to CRLF. That inflated
# email_499_BL.pdf from 2,379 bytes to 2,453 and shifted every offset in its xref
# table, so the parser walked off the end and the app reported a readable document
# as "couldn't be read". 26 of the 58 binary attachments were damaged this way on a
# fresh Windows clone, which is also why the same dataset scored differently on
# different machines.
#
# This changes nothing that is committed. The five deliberately corrupt and scanned
# PDFs behind the `unreadable` review reason (email_511 to email_515) are corrupt in
# the repository itself and still arrive corrupt, which is the point of them.

# Sample documents and anything else read as bytes
*.pdf   binary
*.doc   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.zip   binary

# Trained models: a rewritten byte makes them fail to load
*.joblib binary
*.pkl    binary
*.npy    binary

# Images and fonts
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
